The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
/* Test map close sockets */
for (i = 0; i < 6; i++)
    close(sfd[i]);
```
```c
for (i = 0; i < 6; i++)
    close(sfd[i]);
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because the cleanup phase of the test does not explicitly delete the elements from the sockmaps (`map_fd_tx` and `map_fd_rx`) before closing the sockets. This results in BPF programs and maps related to sockmaps remaining in memory, leading to memory leaks and potential resource exhaustion.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
/* Test map close sockets and empty maps */
for (i = 0; i < 6; i++) {
    bpf_map_delete_elem(map_fd_tx, &i);
    bpf_map_delete_elem(map_fd_rx, &i);
    close(sfd[i]);
}
```
```c
for (i = 0; i < 6; i++) {
    if (map_fd_tx)
        bpf_map_delete_elem(map_fd_tx, &i);
    if (map_fd_rx)
        bpf_map_delete_elem(map_fd_rx, &i);
}
```
Reason:
The fix involves adding explicit deletion of elements from the sockmaps (`bpf_map_delete_elem(map_fd_tx, &i);` and `bpf_map_delete_elem(map_fd_rx, &i);`) before closing the sockets. This ensures that all BPF programs and maps related to sockmaps are properly cleaned up, preventing memory leaks and resource exhaustion after the test ends.

